# next-intl Internationalization Rules

## Architecture Overview

next-intl is the standard i18n library for Next.js App Router (2026). It provides:
- Server Component translations via `getTranslations` (no hooks, no providers)
- Client Component translations via `useTranslations` hook
- Middleware-based locale detection and routing
- Type-safe translation keys with TypeScript
- Built-in pluralization, date/number formatting, and rich text

## Project Structure

```
├── i18n/
│   ├── routing.ts          # Locale list, default locale, prefix strategy
│   ├── request.ts          # Server-side request config (message loading)
│   └── navigation.ts       # Locale-aware Link, redirect, usePathname
├── messages/
│   ├── en.json             # English translations
│   ├── es.json             # Spanish translations
│   └── fr.json             # French translations
├── middleware.ts            # Locale detection and routing (or proxy.ts in Next.js 16+)
├── app/
│   └── [locale]/
│       ├── layout.tsx       # Root locale layout with NextIntlClientProvider
│       ├── page.tsx         # Home page
│       └── dashboard/
│           └── page.tsx     # Nested route
└── next.config.ts           # Uses createNextIntlPlugin
```

## Core Setup Files

### Routing Configuration
```typescript
// i18n/routing.ts
import { defineRouting } from 'next-intl/routing';

export const routing = defineRouting({
  locales: ['en', 'es', 'fr', 'de'] as const,
  defaultLocale: 'en',
  localePrefix: 'always', // 'always' is SEO-preferred — no ambiguity for search engines
});

export type Locale = (typeof routing.locales)[number];
```

### Request Configuration
```typescript
// i18n/request.ts
import { getRequestConfig } from 'next-intl/server';
import { hasLocale } from 'next-intl';
import { routing } from './routing';
import { notFound } from 'next/navigation';

export default getRequestConfig(async ({ requestLocale }) => {
  const requested = await requestLocale;
  const locale = hasLocale(routing.locales, requested) ? requested : routing.defaultLocale;

  if (!hasLocale(routing.locales, requested)) {
    notFound();
  }

  return {
    locale,
    messages: (await import(`../messages/${locale}.json`)).default,
  };
});
```

### Navigation Utilities
```typescript
// i18n/navigation.ts
import { createNavigation } from 'next-intl/navigation';
import { routing } from './routing';

export const { Link, redirect, usePathname, useRouter, getPathname } =
  createNavigation(routing);
```

### Next.js Config
```typescript
// next.config.ts
import createNextIntlPlugin from 'next-intl/plugin';

const withNextIntl = createNextIntlPlugin('./i18n/request.ts');

const nextConfig = {
  // ... other config
};

export default withNextIntl(nextConfig);
```

## Middleware / Proxy

### Next.js 15 (middleware.ts)
```typescript
// middleware.ts
import createMiddleware from 'next-intl/middleware';
import { routing } from './i18n/routing';

export default createMiddleware(routing);

export const config = {
  matcher: ['/((?!api|trpc|_next|_vercel|.*\\..*).*)',],
};
```

### Next.js 16+ (proxy.ts — renamed from middleware.ts)
- Next.js 16 renames `middleware.ts` → `proxy.ts` and the export from `middleware` → `proxy`
- The old file still works but is deprecated
- Run `npx @next/codemod middleware-to-proxy .` to migrate
- The logic is identical; the runtime is Node-only (no Edge)

The middleware handles:
- Reading `Accept-Language` header from the browser
- Cookie-based locale persistence when user switches
- Redirecting `/` → `/en/` (or whichever locale matches)
- Passing the resolved locale to layouts and pages

## Root Locale Layout

```typescript
// app/[locale]/layout.tsx
import { NextIntlClientProvider } from 'next-intl';
import { getMessages, setRequestLocale } from 'next-intl/server';
import { hasLocale } from 'next-intl';
import { notFound } from 'next/navigation';
import { routing } from '@/i18n/routing';

type Props = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  params: Promise<{ locale: string }>;
};

export function generateStaticParams() {
  return routing.locales.map((locale) => ({ locale }));
}

export default async function LocaleLayout({ children, params }: Props) {
  const { locale } = await params;

  if (!hasLocale(routing.locales, locale)) {
    notFound();
  }

  setRequestLocale(locale);

  const messages = await getMessages();

  return (
    <html lang={locale}>
      <body>
        <NextIntlClientProvider messages={messages}>
          {children}
        </NextIntlClientProvider>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

## Server Component Translations

- Use `getTranslations` from `next-intl/server` — no hooks, no providers needed
- Call `setRequestLocale(locale)` at the top of every page and layout for static rendering
- This is the MANDATORY pattern for all pages under `app/[locale]/`

```typescript
// app/[locale]/dashboard/page.tsx
import { getTranslations, setRequestLocale } from 'next-intl/server';

type Props = {
  params: Promise<{ locale: string }>;
};

export default async function DashboardPage({ params }: Props) {
  const { locale } = await params;
  setRequestLocale(locale);

  const t = await getTranslations('Dashboard');

  return (
    <main>
      <h1>{t('title')}</h1>
      <p>{t('description')}</p>
    </main>
  );
}
```

## Client Component Translations

- Use the `useTranslations` hook — requires `NextIntlClientProvider` ancestor
- Only add `'use client'` when the component genuinely needs interactivity or browser APIs
- Keep client components as small leaf nodes; server shell fetches data

```typescript
'use client';

import { useTranslations } from 'next-intl';

export function SubscriptionCard() {
  const t = useTranslations('Billing');

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{t('currentPlan')}</h2>
      <button onClick={() => handleUpgrade()}>
        {t('upgradeCta')}
      </button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

